    <edge source="Explainability methods" target="Designers and troubleshooters of AI-enabled systems" id="0">
      <data key="d4">USES</data>
      <data key="d5">0.9</data>
      <data key="d6">059d812e5b0d7e9221c6eeb3509b86f6d000500e</data>
      <data key="d7">2024</data>
      <data key="d8">I argue that explainability, while indeed a virtue in design, is a virtue aimed primarily at designers and troubleshooters of AI-enabled systems,</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Explainability methods" target="Users and handlers deploying AI-enabled systems" id="0">
      <data key="d4">LACKS</data>
      <data key="d5">0.8</data>
      <data key="d6">059d812e5b0d7e9221c6eeb3509b86f6d000500e</data>
      <data key="d7">2024</data>
      <data key="d8">I argue that explainability will be irrelevant for many current and near-future autonomous systems in the military (which do not incorporate any AI),</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Explainability methods" target="Human–machine teaming" id="0">
      <data key="d4">CONTRADICTS</data>
      <data key="d5">0.5</data>
